  "narrative_body": "On April 17, the Foreign Ministry sent the Storting a 70-year land lease for Norway's Beijing embassy compound, requiring parliamentary approval. A lease that long on diplomatic premises in China is unusual, and the timing matters: Norway restricts Chinese Arctic research, its sovereign wealth fund's ethical guidelines are under review, and Oslo's decision to exclude Israeli bonds from that fund drew US displeasure and fed doubts about its commitment to the China relationship. Formalising a seven-decade presence in Beijing is a deliberate signal that Oslo intends to keep that relationship intact regardless of friction on individual issues.\n\nThe other notable development points in a similar direction. On April 13, Władysław Kosiniak-Kamysz, Poland's deputy prime minister and defence minister, travelled to Oslo to meet Tore Sandvik, Norway's defence minister, at Akershus Fortress. They issued a joint statement but neither minister gave individual press interviews — an unusual format for a high-level defence visit that may reflect operational sensitivity or a wish to control what was said. Poland is one of four countries — alongside the UK, Germany, and France — named as priority partners in Norway's National Security Strategy; both countries also operate the Hanwha K239 Chunmoo multiple-launch rocket system, making joint arms procurement a plausible topic of the closed-door talks.\n\nTogether the two developments confirm Oslo's approach: keep every relationship open, sacrifice none for another. The Beijing lease keeps the China lane open. The Warsaw visit sustains the Central European land-power partnership.\n\nThe domestic picture is unchanged. The minority government still faces the three pressures flagged last week — a threat from the Greens to collapse the coalition over summer budget climate provisions, the Progress Party polling at 17-year highs, and an Epstein-linked intelligence oversight dispute. Andreas Bjelland Eriksen, the climate and environment minister, announced an Oslofjord nitrogen-reduction plan for public consultation before summer; a 30–40% cut in nitrogen inputs is a substantial ecological target, and it gives the Greens a modest sign of progress. But it falls well short of the petroleum and budget climate demands the party has made its red lines. Whether any of these pressures have eased this week could not be confirmed — most of the week's coverage went unmonitored — and the basics remain: a minority government, incompatible coalition demands, and a summer budget approaching.",
